From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU interpreter: FMAC flags and operand normalisation four lanes at a time (SSE2)

What it is for:
  Every FMAC upper instruction normalises its operands, then classifies
  each written lane (result exponent, product exponent for MADD/MSUB/
  OPMSUB, exact model when needed), packs MAC/status/sticky and appends a
  flag entry. Done lane by lane this was about 370 host instructions for
  one four-lane MADD in translated VU code.

The change:
  - fmacFlagsFast(): the per-lane fast paths of normalizeFmacResult,
    calculateFmacProductSticky and updateFmacFlags (patch 0026), checked
    for all four lanes at once with SSE2 compares and movemask. If every
    written lane qualifies, it appends the same flag entry (MAC = the sign
    bits, status S, sticky Z/S of the products) and the per-lane code is
    skipped; otherwise nothing is changed and the per-lane code runs as
    before. dest == 0 always takes the per-lane code (no entry).
  - normalizeOperands4(): normalizeOperand on four lanes (exponent 0 ->
    signed zero, exponent 255 -> signed max), used by execUpperT.
  Non-x86 targets keep the per-lane code only (PS2_VU_FMAC_SIMD 0).

How it was checked:
  The port's VU1 replay (40 workloads) and VU0 replay (60 workloads):
  same checksums and cycle counts, interpreted and translated. ps2xTest
  454/454. A throwaway randomised comparison (2 million FMAC operations
  over every opcode and dest mask, operands near zero, near the range
  limits and in near-cancellation; 1.04 million took the fast path; 8
  million operand lanes) found no difference, compiled plainly and with
  -march=x86-64-v3 -ffp-contract=fast.

